{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1082", "sample_idx": 11, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "He woke to the sound of birds chirping in perfect, looping cadence—too rhythmic, too clean—and the sun cast a golden glow that never shifted its angle. His hand brushed against the grass, and it felt just right—until he noticed the faint pixelation along the blade’s edge when he squinted. The coffee mug beside his bed had a shimmering border, like a UI element half-rendered. He reached for his phone, but the screen flickered with a loading spinner and a message: “Player 7342, welcome back. Quest: ‘Morning Routine’—complete within 12 minutes to unlock Level 2.” His breath hitched. He remembered going to bed last night in his apartment, not this meticulously designed suburban neighborhood with NPCs waving from porches, their smiles too symmetrical, their greetings identical. He tried screaming, but the sound was muffled, as if the world had muted him. A notification popped into his vision: “Emotional response detected. Increasing immersion.”\n\n***\n\nHe walked to the mailbox, heart pounding, and found a letter addressed to him in font he’d seen in game manuals: “Congratulations, you’ve reached the Awakening Threshold.” Inside was a single key and a note: “The exit is real, but you must choose to leave. Most don’t.” He looked around—the trees swayed in perfect sine waves, children’s laughter echoed on a 3.7-second loop, and a dog barked exactly three times before falling silent. He slipped the key into his pocket and turned toward the horizon, where the sky bled into a gradient that didn’t belong to any natural atmosphere. He didn’t know if he was trapped or chosen, but as he took his first step away from his house, the world flickered—not with error, but with anticipation. Somewhere, in a dim room far away, a man in a VR headset opened his eyes, tears streaming, whispering, “I’m back.”", "logprobs": [{"token": "He", "logprob": -0.4743480682373047